# .driftignore — patterns the drift checks should NOT flag.
#
# Copy this to `.driftignore` in your repo clone (it's gitignored locally so
# your personal patterns never get committed):
#
#     cp .driftignore.example .driftignore
#
# Read by BOTH drift checks, with the same matching rule:
#   - scripts/vault-repo-drift-check.sh  (vault vs repo, monthly)
#   - scripts/sync-skills.py             (installed skill copies vs checkout,
#                                         reported at session start)
#
# One pattern per line. Matched as a substring against the path the drift
# check emits (e.g. `rules/voice-style.md`, `scripts/audit-foo.py`,
# `skills/my-private-skill`, `hooks/my-business.sh`).
#
# For sync-skills.py the match is bidirectional and it is the point: an
# ignored skill is left out of the drift report AND skipped by the sync, so a
# skill you have declared personal is never silently overwritten on the next
# `git pull`. Silencing the warning while keeping the overwrite would be worse
# than the warning.
#
# Add a pattern when an item is permanently personal: tied to your name,
# your company, your psychological framework, your domain, or your private
# workflow. Generic patterns are universal and should be contributed
# upstream as PRs instead.
#
# The other common case is a NAME COLLISION: you already had a skill named
# `foo` and the starter bundles its own `foo`. Rename one of them (two skills
# cannot share a slug), then ignore the survivor's path here so the checker
# stops comparing two unrelated skills and calling them "diverged".

# --- Examples (delete or edit) ---
# rules/my-voice-style.md
# rules/my-company-brand-guide.md
# scripts/my-business-digest.sh
# skills/my-private-skill
# hooks/my-business.sh

# --- Patterns that are universal-personal and always safe to ignore ---
# (uncomment if you don't want them flagged)
# scripts/__pycache__
